# genomedk-jobs skill — per-project config.
#
# Copy this to `hpc.env` (or `.hpc/hpc.env`) at your PROJECT root and fill in.
# The wrappers walk up from the current directory to find it, so it is found
# from anywhere inside the project. Plain `KEY=value` lines (no spaces around
# `=`), read as inert data — never sourced or executed — so a committed config
# cannot run code on the machine that loads it, and $VARs stay literal. No
# secrets live here, so it is safe to commit and share with collaborators.

# --- cluster identity ---------------------------------------------------

# SSH alias for the login node, defined in ~/.ssh/config with
# ControlMaster/ControlPersist. See reference/ssh_setup.md.
HPC_HOST=genomedk

# Your username on the cluster (used to filter `squeue`).
HPC_USER=your-cluster-username

# SLURM account to charge.
HPC_ACCOUNT=your_slurm_account

# --- the write boundary -------------------------------------------------

# THE most important setting. Every remote write (push, submit, fetch) is
# refused unless it lands under this directory. Must be an absolute path you
# own on the shared filesystem. On GenomeDK these live under
# /faststorage/project/<PROJECT>/...
HPC_REMOTE_ROOT=/faststorage/project/YOUR_PROJECT/you/your-subdir

# --- defaults (override per-job on the command line) --------------------

# Default SLURM partition. A single name (normal) or a comma-list
# (gpu-short,gpu-l40s) so SLURM takes whichever can run the job first; the
# walltime ceiling is then the MOST PERMISSIVE partition in the list, since the
# job simply runs in one where it fits (a global 7-day cap applies on top).
# `normal` is GenomeDK's default CPU partition (7 d); `short` caps at 12 h. For
# a GPU job, set a GPU partition here AND pass `--gpus N` to hpc_submit.py (it
# requests 0 GPUs by default). GenomeDK GPU partitions: gpu-l40s (7 d) and
# gpu-short (2 h) are L40S 48 GB; gpu-h200 (7 d) is H200 141 GB.
# Run `gnodes` on the login node for the live list. Docs: https://genome.au.dk/docs/
HPC_PARTITION=normal

# Email for SLURM END/FAIL notifications. Leave blank to disable mail.
HPC_MAIL_USER=you@example.com

# Subdirectory under HPC_REMOTE_ROOT that holds your pushed code and is the
# working directory for jobs.
HPC_CODE_SUBDIR=repo

# What `hpc_push.sh` (called with no arguments) syncs up, space-separated,
# relative to the project root. Include your environment lockfile here: the
# skill assumes pixi (pixi.toml + pixi.lock), installed on the login node and
# run via `pixi run` — see the Dependencies section in SKILL.md. Swap these for
# environment.yml / requirements.txt if you use conda / pip instead.
HPC_PUSH_PATHS="src scripts pixi.toml pixi.lock"

# How a pushed path's directory components are treated. Unset (the default),
# rsync strips them, so only each source's BASENAME appears remotely: a
# top-level entry like `src` lands at $HPC_CODE_SUBDIR/src as expected, but a
# nested one like `conf/params.yaml` lands at $HPC_CODE_SUBDIR/params.yaml, NOT
# .../conf/params.yaml. Push prints the resolved mapping and warns when it drops
# a component. Set to 1 to preserve the paths instead (rsync -R), which is
# usually what you want if HPC_PUSH_PATHS has nested entries.
# HPC_PUSH_RELATIVE=1

# Shell lines injected into every job before your command runs (environment
# activation, caches, etc.). They run inside the job, where $REMOTE_ROOT is
# already defined, so reference $REMOTE_ROOT (not $HPC_REMOTE_ROOT): it is
# expanded on the compute node, never here (this file is read as inert data, so
# $VARs stay literal regardless of quoting; any one surrounding quote pair is
# stripped). These lines run under `set -e`, so append `|| true` to any whose
# non-zero exit is expected (e.g. a `module load` that warns).
# Example for pixi on GenomeDK:
# HPC_JOB_SETUP='export PIXI_CACHE_DIR=$REMOTE_ROOT/.pixi-cache; export PATH=$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH; export TORCH_HOME=$REMOTE_ROOT/.torch-cache'

# --- local bookkeeping --------------------------------------------------

# Project root on your machine. Defaults to the directory containing hpc.env,
# which is why hpc.env belongs AT your project root: HPC_PUSH_PATHS is resolved
# against this, so an hpc.env one directory up silently makes every pushed path
# resolve into the wrong tree. Set this explicitly if the config must live
# elsewhere (it is also where .hpc_audit.log and .hpc_root_verified are written).
# HPC_LOCAL_ROOT=.

# Where the JSONL audit trail is written locally. Defaults to
# $HPC_LOCAL_ROOT/.hpc_audit.log.
# HPC_AUDIT_LOG=.hpc_audit.log

# --- safety toggles (usually leave unset) -------------------------------

# Set to 1 to keep a recoverable copy of any remote file a push overwrites,
# under $HPC_REMOTE_ROOT/.hpc_backups/<timestamp>. Push never deletes either way.
# HPC_PUSH_BACKUP=1

# Which rsync binary the wrappers run. Defaults to whatever `rsync` is on PATH.
# macOS 15+ ships openrsync as /usr/bin/rsync — a different implementation that
# usually wins over a Homebrew GNU rsync in PATH order. Every option this skill
# passes behaves identically on both, so you normally leave this unset; pin it
# only to settle a divergence. NOTE this file is shared, and an absolute path
# from one laptop will not exist on a collaborator's machine (the wrappers say so
# rather than failing with a bare 127), so prefer setting it in the environment.
# HPC_RSYNC=/opt/homebrew/bin/rsync

# The first push/submit verifies over SSH that HPC_REMOTE_ROOT is owned by you,
# to catch a mistyped path pointing at another project. Set to 1 only if the
# root is a genuinely shared directory you do not own.
# HPC_ALLOW_UNOWNED_ROOT=1
